// Inherent methods on the built-in sequences.
//
// `.len()` is **not** compiler syntax. It is an ordinary inherent method on the
// primitive types, declared here — an `impl` with no trait, on a type no other
// package is allowed to write one for — and marked `#intrinsic` (§6.4), so the
// compiler supplies what it does. That is what makes `a.len()` on a `[3]i32`,
// `s.len()` on a `[]i32`, and a future `Vector.len()` one spelling with one
// meaning, instead of a special case the compiler carries.
//
// The coherence rule is why these can only live in `core`: an inherent impl may
// only be written in the package that defines the type, and the structural types
// belong to `core` (§4.9).
//
// `Self` inside these impls is the structural target: `[]T` in the first, `[N]T`
// in the second. On a `[N]T` whose `N` is known, `len` folds to the literal
// count at compile time, so the inlined call costs nothing; on a `[]T` it reads
// the slice header's length.

// The element count of an array or slice. This is the second intrinsic whose
// signature cannot state its own rule: `T` is unbounded here and the compiler
// rejects an argument that is neither an array nor a slice, because no bound in
// the language says "one of the two built-in sequences".
@public len :: #intrinsic("len") func <T> (x: T) -> usize

// The method **is** the intrinsic, rather than a body that calls one. Writing it
// the other way — `len :: func (self: *Self) -> usize { return len(self) }` —
// looks like it forwards to the namespace's `len` above and does not: inside an
// impl, `len` resolves to the impl's own member, so the body called itself.
//
// Marking the member `#intrinsic` is also the more honest spelling of what these
// are. There was never a body to write: `#intrinsic` means the compiler supplies
// one (§6.4), and a forwarding call was only ever a way of naming which one.
impl <T> []T {
  len :: #intrinsic("len") func (self: *Self) -> usize
}

impl <T, const N: usize> [N]T {
  len :: #intrinsic("len") func (self: *Self) -> usize
}
